      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-32667",
      ":question/problem": "If $x=2{y}^{2}+7y-\\frac{3}{y},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+7y-\\frac{3}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+7y-3{y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+7\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4y+7+3{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =4y+7+\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{3}+7{y}^{2}+3}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4{y}^{3}+7{y}^{2}+3}.\n$$"
    },
