      ":question/id": "e92e83a8-1c00-5738-9fc2-4ba6c65f2b58",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-341626",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $x=4\\sqrt{y}+3{y}^{5},$ express $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $y.$\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | $2\\sqrt{y}\\,+${{field-2}} |",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4\\sqrt{y}+3{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{1/2}+3{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{1/2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =2{y}^{-1/2}+15{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{y}}+15{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{y}}{y}+15{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{y}+15{y}^{5}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{y}+15{y}^{5}}.\n$$"
    },
