      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-170132",
      ":question/problem": "If $x=3{y}^{3}-4{y}^{5},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{3}-4{y}^{5}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3})-4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{5}) \\\\  & =9{y}^{2}-20{y}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{9{y}^{2}-20{y}^{4}}.\n$$"
